# Performance Optimization

## React.memo Usage

### When to Use React.memo

Use `React.memo` for components that:
- Re-render frequently with the same props
- Are expensive to render (complex calculations, large lists)
- Are passed as props to frequently re-rendering parents

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Expensive component that rarely changes
const ExpensiveChart = React.memo(({ data }: { data: ChartData }) => {
  const processedData = useMemo(() => processChartData(data), [data]);
  return <Chart data={processedData} />;
});

// ❌ BAD - Simple component, memo overhead not worth it
const SimpleLabel = React.memo(({ text }: { text: string }) => {
  return <span>{text}</span>;
});
```

### Custom Comparison Function

Use custom comparison for complex props:

```typescript
const UserCard = React.memo(
  ({ user }: { user: User }) => {
    return <div>{user.name}</div>;
  },
  (prevProps, nextProps) => {
    // Only re-render if user ID or name changes
    return (
      prevProps.user.id === nextProps.user.id &&
      prevProps.user.name === nextProps.user.name
    );
  }
);
```

## useMemo and useCallback

### useMemo for Expensive Calculations

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Expensive calculation memoized; use toSorted() (or spread first)
// to avoid mutating the prop array in place.
const ExpensiveComponent = ({ items }: { items: Item[] }) => {
  const sortedItems = useMemo(() => {
    return items.toSorted((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority);
    // Or: [...items].sort((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority)
  }, [items]);

  return <ItemList items={sortedItems} />;
};

// ❌ BAD - Recalculates on every render AND mutates the prop array in place
const ExpensiveComponent = ({ items }: { items: Item[] }) => {
  const sortedItems = items.sort((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority);
  return <ItemList items={sortedItems} />;
};
```

### useCallback for Stable Function References

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Stable callback reference
const ParentComponent = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(c => c + 1);
  }, []); // Empty deps - function never changes

  return <ExpensiveChildComponent onClick={handleClick} />;
};

// ❌ BAD - New function on every render
const ParentComponent = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleClick = () => {
    setCount(c => c + 1);
  };

  return <ExpensiveChildComponent onClick={handleClick} />;
};
```

### When NOT to Use useMemo/useCallback

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Overhead not worth it for simple operations
const sum = useMemo(() => a + b, [a, b]);

// ✅ GOOD - Simple operations don't need memoization
const sum = a + b;

// ❌ BAD - Function only used once, no need for useCallback
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  console.log('clicked');
}, []);

// ✅ GOOD - Only memoize if passed to memoized child
const handleClick = () => {
  console.log('clicked');
};
```

## Code Splitting and Lazy Loading

### Route-Level Code Splitting

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Lazy load route components
import { lazy } from 'react';

const DashboardPage = lazy(() => import('@/pages/DashboardPage'));
const SettingsPage = lazy(() => import('@/pages/SettingsPage'));

// In router
export const Route = createFileRoute('/dashboard')({
  component: DashboardPage,
});
```

### Component-Level Code Splitting

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Lazy load heavy components
const HeavyChart = lazy(() => import('@/components/charts/HeavyChart'));

function Dashboard() {
  const [showChart, setShowChart] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setShowChart(true)}>Show Chart</button>
      {showChart && (
        <Suspense fallback={<ChartSkeleton />}>
          <HeavyChart />
        </Suspense>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Always Provide Suspense Boundaries

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Suspense boundary for lazy components
<Suspense fallback={<LoadingSpinner />}>
  <LazyComponent />
</Suspense>

// ❌ BAD - No fallback, poor UX
<LazyComponent />
```

## Virtual Scrolling

Use virtual scrolling for large lists:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Virtual scrolling for 1000+ items
import { useVirtualizer } from '@tanstack/react-virtual';

function LargeList({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
  const parentRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const virtualizer = useVirtualizer({
    count: items.length,
    getScrollElement: () => parentRef.current,
    estimateSize: () => 50,
  });

  return (
    <div ref={parentRef} className="h-96 overflow-auto">
      <div style={{ height: `${virtualizer.getTotalSize()}px` }}>
        {virtualizer.getVirtualItems().map((virtualItem) => (
          <div
            key={virtualItem.key}
            style={{
              position: 'absolute',
              top: 0,
              left: 0,
              width: '100%',
              height: `${virtualItem.size}px`,
              transform: `translateY(${virtualItem.start}px)`,
            }}
          >
            <ItemCard item={items[virtualItem.index]} />
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Debouncing and Throttling

### Debounce Expensive Operations

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Debounce search input
import { useDebouncedValue } from '@/hooks/useDebouncedValue';

function SearchInput() {
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');
  const debouncedSearchTerm = useDebouncedValue(searchTerm, 300);

  const { data } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['search', debouncedSearchTerm],
    queryFn: () => searchService.search(debouncedSearchTerm),
    enabled: !!debouncedSearchTerm,
  });

  return (
    <input
      value={searchTerm}
      onChange={(e) => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)}
      placeholder="Search..."
    />
  );
}
```

### Throttle Scroll Events

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Throttle scroll handler
import { throttle } from 'lodash-es';

function ScrollableList() {
  const handleScroll = useMemo(
    () =>
      throttle((e: React.UIEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {
        // Handle scroll
      }, 100),
    []
  );

  return <div onScroll={handleScroll}>...</div>;
}
```

## Image Optimization

### Lazy Load Images

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Native lazy loading
<img
  src={imageUrl}
  alt={description}
  loading="lazy"
  decoding="async"
/>

// ✅ GOOD - Use WebP format when possible
<picture>
  <source srcSet={imageWebP} type="image/webp" />
  <img src={imageJPG} alt={description} loading="lazy" />
</picture>
```

## React Query Optimization

### Selective Data Fetching

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Only fetch what's needed
const { data: user } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['user', id],
  queryFn: () => userService.getUser(id),
  select: (user) => ({
    id: user.id,
    name: user.name,
    // Only select needed fields
  }),
});

// ❌ BAD - Fetching entire user object when only name is needed
const { data: user } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['user', id],
  queryFn: () => userService.getUser(id),
});
```

### Stale Time Configuration

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Configure stale time based on data freshness needs
const { data } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['users'],
  queryFn: getUsers,
  staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes - data doesn't change often
});

const { data: notifications } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['notifications'],
  queryFn: getNotifications,
  staleTime: 0, // Always fresh - real-time data
});
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Measure Before Optimizing

```typescript
// Use React DevTools Profiler to identify bottlenecks
// Don't optimize prematurely
```

### 2. Optimize Re-renders, Not Renders

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Prevent unnecessary re-renders
const MemoizedChild = React.memo(ChildComponent);

// ❌ BAD - Trying to prevent initial render (impossible)
```

### 3. Use React DevTools Profiler

- Identify components that re-render unnecessarily
- Measure render times
- Find performance bottlenecks

### 4. Bundle Analysis

```bash
# Analyze bundle size
pnpm build --analyze

# Check for large dependencies
pnpm why <package-name>
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Overusing React.memo

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Memoizing everything
const Button = React.memo(({ onClick, children }) => (
  <button onClick={onClick}>{children}</button>
));

// ✅ GOOD - Only memoize when needed
const Button = ({ onClick, children }) => (
  <button onClick={onClick}>{children}</button>
);
```

### ❌ Memoizing Primitives

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - No benefit
const count = useMemo(() => 0, []);

// ✅ GOOD - Just use the value
const count = 0;
```

### ❌ Creating Objects/Arrays in Render

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - New object on every render
<ChildComponent style={{ margin: 10 }} />

// ✅ GOOD - Stable reference
const style = useMemo(() => ({ margin: 10 }), []);
<ChildComponent style={style} />
```
